From 4272058b7a610467f0d1c4ad220849af571af56a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <michieleghuizen@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 13:25:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/14] fs: answer statx() without leaving rcu-walk when the
 filesystem allows it

Every stat of a path today ends with complete_walk(): the walk that
found the dentry under rcu_read_lock() takes a reference on it
(lockref_get_not_dead(), an atomic cmpxchg on the dentry's cacheline),
bumps the mount count, issues a full barrier, leaves RCU, hands the
struct path to vfs_getattr(), and then path_put() undoes it all with a
second atomic on the same cacheline. The getattr in between reads a few
dozen bytes out of the inode.

On a dentry that many CPUs stat concurrently those two atomics are the
only part of the syscall that does not scale. They are also the only
part that writes to shared memory at all: the rest of the walk is reads
protected by sequence counts, and the getattr is reads. The references
exist to give ->getattr and the LSM a pinned path, and nothing else.

RCU already keeps the dentry, the inode and the mount in memory for as
long as the walk is inside rcu_read_lock(), and the dentry's sequence
count already tells the walk whether the dentry it looked at is still
the same object bound to the same inode; that is how every component
before the last one is handled. This patch applies the same rule to the
operation at the end.

filename_lookup_op() takes a struct path_op with two callbacks. After
the walk arrives at the last component still in rcu-walk, it calls
->rcu on the unpinned path, then rechecks the dentry's seqcount and
mount_lock. If both are unchanged the answer stands, whatever it is;
a success or a hard error computed from a dentry that was renamed or
killed in the meantime is discarded alike. If ->rcu returns -ECHILD, or
the walk had already fallen out of rcu-walk, it does what
complete_walk() does today and calls ->ref with references held, so a
filesystem that cannot answer in RCU pays for one walk, not two. The
existing -ECHILD and -ESTALE retries of filename_lookup() apply
unchanged, and -ESTALE from the operation is retried with LOOKUP_REVAL
as callers that loop on retry_estale() do by hand today.

Three things complete_walk() would do are left to the ref-walk side
rather than reproduced in RCU: the scoped-lookup containment check,
->d_weak_revalidate() after a jump, and audit_inode(), which allocates
when an audit context is live. All three are detected before ->rcu is
tried.

vfs_statx() becomes a filename_lookup_op() with vfs_statx_rcu() and
vfs_statx_ref(). The ref half is the old vfs_statx_path(). The rcu half
requires the inode to carry IOP_GETATTR_RCU, refuses block devices
(bdev_statx() goes to the block layer), asks the LSM through the
inode_getattr_rcu hook added two patches ago, and calls ->getattr with
AT_GETATTR_RCU in query_flags.

IOP_GETATTR_RCU is the filesystem's promise about its ->getattr:
called under rcu_read_lock() with nothing pinned, it does not sleep,
takes no reference it cannot drop without sleeping, has no side effect
beyond filling the kstat, and returns -ECHILD if it needs any of that.
The inode it is handed may be in the middle of eviction or, on
filesystems that recycle inodes, of reuse; its memory is RCU-protected
and the result is thrown away, but the implementation must tolerate
reading it. ->getattr implementations that read inode fields and take
spinlocks qualify; anything that talks to a server, the disk or the
block layer does not. The following patches opt ext4, xfs and btrfs in;
tmpfs is left out on purpose, because shmem_getattr() reconciles the
inode's block accounting as a side effect and that must not run against
an inode being evicted.

vfs_getattr_nosec() used to strip query_flags down to the sync type
before calling the filesystem; it now lets AT_GETATTR_RCU through. The
bit is kernel-internal, outside the range statx() accepts from
userspace, and defined next to struct kstat.

What this saves per stat on an opted-in filesystem, when the walk stays
in rcu-walk (the common case on a warm dcache): the lockref_get_not_dead()
and lockref_put_return() on the dentry, the mount count increment and
decrement, the smp_mb() in __legitimize_mnt(), and the rcu_read_unlock()/
lock pair. On a dentry stat'ed by many CPUs it removes every write to
shared memory from the syscall. It also makes the n+1 pattern behind
ls -l, find and every recursive tool - readdir followed by one statx
per entry - a sequence of read-only operations on the directory's
dentries.

Nothing changes for filesystems that do not set the flag, for
statx(fd, "", AT_EMPTY_PATH) and fstat(), which never walked, for
lookups with a live audit context, for scoped lookups, or for kernels
with an LSM that hooks inode_getattr without providing the rcu variant.
---
 Documentation/filesystems/porting.rst |  15 ++++
 Documentation/filesystems/vfs.rst     |  10 +++
 fs/internal.h                         |  13 ++++
 fs/namei.c                            | 101 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 fs/stat.c                             | 101 ++++++++++++++++++++------
 include/linux/fs.h                    |   9 +++
 include/linux/stat.h                  |   8 ++
 7 files changed, 236 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/filesystems/porting.rst b/Documentation/filesystems/porting.rst
index 60880eb0c49d..50cd5c9a3da0 100644
--- a/Documentation/filesystems/porting.rst
+++ b/Documentation/filesystems/porting.rst
@@ -1409,3 +1409,18 @@ use only if you have no alternative.
 The .create inode_operation no longer receives the 'excl' arg.  It must
 always assume the file does not already exist.  If the filesystem needs
 to be involved in non-exclusive create, it should provide atomic_open.
+
+---
+
+**recommended**
+
+stat() no longer needs to leave rcu-walk if the filesystem lets it.  Set
+IOP_GETATTR_RCU in ->i_opflags when installing ->i_op on an inode whose
+->getattr() can run under rcu_read_lock(): no sleeping, no references it
+cannot drop without sleeping, no side effects beyond filling the kstat, and
+-ECHILD when it cannot answer under those rules.  It is then called with
+AT_GETATTR_RCU in query_flags from statx() and friends, with nothing pinned;
+the inode may be evicted or reused under it and the result is discarded if the
+dentry changed.  ->getattr() implementations that only read the inode and
+take spinlocks qualify; anything that goes to the server, the disk, or the
+block layer does not.  Not setting the flag keeps the old behaviour exactly.
diff --git a/Documentation/filesystems/vfs.rst b/Documentation/filesystems/vfs.rst
index d3a93eec3945..dd658cde23cd 100644
--- a/Documentation/filesystems/vfs.rst
+++ b/Documentation/filesystems/vfs.rst
@@ -583,6 +583,16 @@ otherwise noted.
 	called by the VFS to get attributes of a file.  This method is
 	called by stat(2) and related system calls.
 
+	If the filesystem sets IOP_GETATTR_RCU in inode->i_opflags, this
+	method may also be called in rcu-walk with AT_GETATTR_RCU set in
+	query_flags.  It must then not sleep and must not take a reference
+	it cannot drop without sleeping; if it needs to do either it
+	returns -ECHILD and is called again the ordinary way.  Nothing is
+	pinned in that mode: the inode may be evicted or reused under the
+	call (its memory is RCU-protected), and the VFS discards the result
+	if the dentry changed, so the method must have no side effects
+	beyond filling in the kstat.
+
 ``listxattr``
 	called by the VFS to list all extended attributes for a given
 	file.  This method is called by the listxattr(2) system call.
diff --git a/fs/internal.h b/fs/internal.h
index 438581c429f9..ea28771abb5c 100644
--- a/fs/internal.h
+++ b/fs/internal.h
@@ -53,6 +53,19 @@ extern int finish_clean_context(struct fs_context *fc);
  */
 extern int filename_lookup(int dfd, struct filename *name, unsigned flags,
 			   struct path *path, const struct path *root);
+/*
+ * An operation on the result of a path lookup that can sometimes be done
+ * without leaving rcu-walk. ->rcu is called under rcu_read_lock() with
+ * nothing pinned and returns -ECHILD to ask for ->ref instead; ->ref is
+ * called with references held, as if after filename_lookup(). Whatever
+ * either returns is passed to the caller once the walk is torn down.
+ */
+struct path_op {
+	int (*rcu)(const struct path *path, void *arg);
+	int (*ref)(const struct path *path, void *arg);
+};
+int filename_lookup_op(int dfd, struct filename *name, unsigned flags,
+		       const struct path_op *op, void *arg);
 int filename_rmdir(int dfd, struct filename *name);
 int filename_unlinkat(int dfd, struct filename *name);
 int may_linkat(struct mnt_idmap *idmap, const struct path *link);
diff --git a/fs/namei.c b/fs/namei.c
index 0cff9ff187db..457b8a8f5e77 100644
--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -2831,6 +2831,107 @@ static int path_lookupat(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags, struct path *path
 	return err;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Like path_lookupat(), but instead of handing the result back pinned,
+ * apply @op to it in place - in rcu-walk if it will go, in ref-walk if not.
+ *
+ * The point is what is *not* done on the fast path: no complete_walk(), so no
+ * lockref_get_not_dead() on the dentry, no mount count, no smp_mb(), and no
+ * path_put() afterwards. For an operation that only reads the inode that is
+ * the entire cost of the syscall that is shared with other CPUs.
+ *
+ * What replaces the references is the same thing that protects the walk
+ * itself: rcu_read_lock() keeps the dentry, inode and mount in memory, and
+ * the dentry's seqcount plus mount_lock, sampled before and checked after,
+ * tell us whether they were the same objects throughout. If not, or if
+ * ->rcu declines with -ECHILD, we drop out to ref-walk from where we stand -
+ * complete_walk() already knows how to do that - and call ->ref instead, so
+ * a filesystem that cannot play does not pay for a second walk.
+ *
+ * Two of complete_walk()'s duties are left to the ref-walk side rather than
+ * reproduced here: the scoped-lookup containment check (rare, and
+ * path_is_under() wants locks), and ->d_weak_revalidate() after a jump
+ * (network filesystems, which do not opt in to begin with). Audit too: with a
+ * live audit context, audit_inode() allocates, so it is ref-walk only.
+ */
+static int path_lookupat_op(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags,
+			    const struct path_op *op, void *arg)
+{
+	const char *s = path_init(nd, flags);
+	int err;
+
+	while (!(err = link_path_walk(s, nd)) &&
+	       (s = lookup_last(nd)) != NULL)
+		;
+	if (!err && unlikely(nd->flags & LOOKUP_DIRECTORY) &&
+	    !d_can_lookup(nd->path.dentry))
+		err = -ENOTDIR;
+	if (err)
+		goto out;
+
+	if (likely(nd->flags & LOOKUP_RCU) &&
+	    likely(audit_dummy_context()) &&
+	    likely(!(nd->flags & LOOKUP_IS_SCOPED)) &&
+	    likely(!(nd->state & ND_JUMPED) ||
+		   !(nd->path.dentry->d_flags & DCACHE_OP_WEAK_REVALIDATE))) {
+		err = op->rcu(&nd->path, arg);
+		/*
+		 * Whatever ->rcu concluded, it concluded about the objects
+		 * nd->path pointed at when the walk arrived here. Make sure
+		 * they are still those objects; a success or a hard error
+		 * computed from a dentry that has since been renamed away
+		 * or killed is equally worthless.
+		 */
+		if (read_seqcount_retry(&nd->path.dentry->d_seq, nd->seq) ||
+		    read_seqretry(&mount_lock, nd->m_seq))
+			err = -ECHILD;
+		if (err != -ECHILD)
+			goto out;
+	}
+
+	err = complete_walk(nd);
+	if (!err) {
+		audit_inode(nd->name, nd->path.dentry, 0);
+		err = op->ref(&nd->path, arg);
+	}
+out:
+	terminate_walk(nd);
+	return err;
+}
+
+/**
+ * filename_lookup_op - look a path up and apply an operation to the result
+ * @dfd: directory the lookup is relative to, or AT_FDCWD
+ * @name: the path
+ * @flags: LOOKUP_* flags; LOOKUP_RCU is added for the first attempt
+ * @op: what to do with the result, see struct path_op
+ * @arg: passed through to @op
+ *
+ * Equivalent to filename_lookup() followed by op->ref() and path_put(), with
+ * the same -ECHILD and -ESTALE retries, except that when the whole walk stayed
+ * in rcu-walk and op->rcu() can answer, no reference is ever taken.
+ *
+ * -ESTALE from @op is retried with LOOKUP_REVAL like -ESTALE from the walk,
+ * which is what callers that loop on retry_estale() do by hand today.
+ */
+int filename_lookup_op(int dfd, struct filename *name, unsigned flags,
+		       const struct path_op *op, void *arg)
+{
+	struct nameidata nd;
+	int retval;
+
+	if (IS_ERR(name))
+		return PTR_ERR(name);
+	set_nameidata(&nd, dfd, name, NULL);
+	retval = path_lookupat_op(&nd, flags | LOOKUP_RCU, op, arg);
+	if (unlikely(retval == -ECHILD))
+		retval = path_lookupat_op(&nd, flags, op, arg);
+	if (unlikely(retval == -ESTALE))
+		retval = path_lookupat_op(&nd, flags | LOOKUP_REVAL, op, arg);
+	restore_nameidata();
+	return retval;
+}
+
 int filename_lookup(int dfd, struct filename *name, unsigned flags,
 		    struct path *path, const struct path *root)
 {
diff --git a/fs/stat.c b/fs/stat.c
index c461c3054234..9754019559e8 100644
--- a/fs/stat.c
+++ b/fs/stat.c
@@ -186,7 +186,7 @@ int vfs_getattr_nosec(const struct path *path, struct kstat *stat,
 
 	memset(stat, 0, sizeof(*stat));
 	stat->result_mask |= STATX_BASIC_STATS;
-	query_flags &= AT_STATX_SYNC_TYPE;
+	query_flags &= AT_STATX_SYNC_TYPE | AT_GETATTR_RCU;
 
 	/* allow the fs to override these if it really wants to */
 	/* SB_NOATIME means filesystem supplies dummy atime value */
@@ -293,13 +293,9 @@ static int statx_lookup_flags(int flags)
 	return lookup_flags;
 }
 
-static int vfs_statx_path(const struct path *path, int flags, struct kstat *stat,
-			  u32 request_mask)
+static void statx_fill_mount(const struct path *path, struct kstat *stat,
+			     u32 request_mask)
 {
-	int error = vfs_getattr(path, stat, request_mask, flags);
-	if (error)
-		return error;
-
 	if (request_mask & STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE) {
 		stat->mnt_id = real_mount(path->mnt)->mnt_id_unique;
 		stat->result_mask |= STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE;
@@ -311,9 +307,72 @@ static int vfs_statx_path(const struct path *path, int flags, struct kstat *stat
 	if (path_mounted(path))
 		stat->attributes |= STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT;
 	stat->attributes_mask |= STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT;
+}
+
+static int vfs_statx_path(const struct path *path, int flags, struct kstat *stat,
+			  u32 request_mask)
+{
+	int error = vfs_getattr(path, stat, request_mask, flags);
+	if (error)
+		return error;
+
+	statx_fill_mount(path, stat, request_mask);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+struct statx_args {
+	struct kstat *stat;
+	u32 request_mask;
+	int flags;
+};
+
+static int vfs_statx_ref(const struct path *path, void *data)
+{
+	struct statx_args *a = data;
+
+	return vfs_statx_path(path, a->flags, a->stat, a->request_mask);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The rcu-walk half of vfs_statx_path(): the same result, produced without
+ * anything pinned. Only for filesystems whose ->getattr() has opted in with
+ * IOP_GETATTR_RCU, and never for block devices, whose bdev_statx() goes to the
+ * block layer. -ECHILD sends filename_lookup_op() to vfs_statx_ref().
+ */
+static int vfs_statx_rcu(const struct path *path, void *data)
+{
+	struct statx_args *a = data;
+	struct inode *inode = d_backing_inode(path->dentry);
+	int error;
+
+	/*
+	 * The walk saw a positive dentry, but nothing is pinned: an unlink
+	 * since then has cleared d_inode under a d_seq write section, and the
+	 * caller's seqcount check will catch it. Don't dereference NULL first.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(!inode))
+		return -ECHILD;
+	if (!(inode->i_opflags & IOP_GETATTR_RCU) || unlikely(S_ISBLK(inode->i_mode)))
+		return -ECHILD;
+
+	error = security_inode_getattr_rcu(path);
+	if (error)
+		return error;
+
+	error = vfs_getattr_nosec(path, a->stat, a->request_mask,
+				  a->flags | AT_GETATTR_RCU);
+	if (error)
+		return error;
+
+	statx_fill_mount(path, a->stat, a->request_mask);
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static const struct path_op vfs_statx_op = {
+	.rcu = vfs_statx_rcu,
+	.ref = vfs_statx_ref,
+};
+
 static int vfs_statx_fd(int fd, int flags, struct kstat *stat,
 			  u32 request_mask)
 {
@@ -341,25 +400,25 @@ static int vfs_statx_fd(int fd, int flags, struct kstat *stat,
 static int vfs_statx(int dfd, struct filename *filename, int flags,
 	      struct kstat *stat, u32 request_mask)
 {
-	struct path path;
-	unsigned int lookup_flags = statx_lookup_flags(flags);
-	int error;
+	struct statx_args args = {
+		.stat = stat,
+		.request_mask = request_mask,
+		.flags = flags,
+	};
 
 	if (flags & ~(AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW | AT_NO_AUTOMOUNT | AT_EMPTY_PATH |
 		      AT_STATX_SYNC_TYPE))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-retry:
-	error = filename_lookup(dfd, filename, lookup_flags, &path, NULL);
-	if (error)
-		return error;
-	error = vfs_statx_path(&path, flags, stat, request_mask);
-	path_put(&path);
-	if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
-		lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
-		goto retry;
-	}
-	return error;
+	/*
+	 * Looks the path up and runs vfs_statx_rcu() on it before leaving
+	 * rcu-walk if it can, vfs_statx_ref() with references if it cannot.
+	 * -ESTALE from either the walk or the filesystem is retried with
+	 * LOOKUP_REVAL inside, which is what the retry_estale() loop that used
+	 * to be here did.
+	 */
+	return filename_lookup_op(dfd, filename, statx_lookup_flags(flags),
+				  &vfs_statx_op, &args);
 }
 
 int vfs_fstatat(int dfd, const char __user *filename,
diff --git a/include/linux/fs.h b/include/linux/fs.h
index f9d1e05e8ae6..69846ffea3c5 100644
--- a/include/linux/fs.h
+++ b/include/linux/fs.h
@@ -627,6 +627,15 @@ is_uncached_acl(struct posix_acl *acl)
 #define IOP_CACHED_LINK		0x0040
 #define IOP_FASTPERM_MAY_EXEC	0x0080
 #define IOP_FLCTX		0x0100
+/*
+ * Set by the filesystem when ->getattr() can be called under rcu_read_lock()
+ * with AT_GETATTR_RCU in query_flags: it then may not sleep, may not take a
+ * reference it cannot drop without sleeping, and returns -ECHILD if it needs
+ * either. Nothing is pinned: the inode may be evicted or reused under it (its
+ * memory is RCU-protected) and the caller discards the result if the dentry
+ * changed. So it must have no side effects beyond filling the kstat.
+ */
+#define IOP_GETATTR_RCU		0x0200
 
 /*
  * Inode state bits.  Protected by inode->i_lock
diff --git a/include/linux/stat.h b/include/linux/stat.h
index 9c5709132862..88aa9ec28962 100644
--- a/include/linux/stat.h
+++ b/include/linux/stat.h
@@ -19,6 +19,14 @@
 #include <linux/time.h>
 #include <linux/uidgid.h>
 
+/*
+ * Kernel-internal query_flags bit for ->getattr(): the call is being made in
+ * rcu-walk, see IOP_GETATTR_RCU. Chosen outside the range userspace can pass
+ * in statx() flags; vfs_getattr_nosec() masks everything else out before the
+ * filesystem sees query_flags.
+ */
+#define AT_GETATTR_RCU		0x80000000
+
 struct kstat {
 	u32		result_mask;	/* What fields the user got */
 	umode_t		mode;
-- 
